[Music]  [Music]  i'm in favor as a long run goal  not something achieved tomorrow but  something to keep our eye on  of trying to move to a condition where  we eradicate race from social judgment  [Music]  race is here and what we do with it is  completely up to us  i am of the belief that what we will not  do  is eliminate it  [Music]  you're either part of the journey for  change  or you're part of a system that wants to  keep the status quo  hello we want to see the end of  discrimination  on the grounds of race to this end in  2018  france unanimously voted to remove the  word race from its constitution  endorsing the view that race should play  no part in any decision or action  yet at the same time many want to assert  their right to be proud of their  heritage  and see race as a defining part of their  identity  should we be seeking to eradicate race  from social judgments  and as a result must we give up  attachment to being part of any  particular racial group  or does ignoring race cover up historic  injustices  and disregard inherent differences in  identity  well it's a fascinating and extremely  timely debate  and i'm very glad to say that we've got  three outstanding speakers to join us  this evening first we have professor  glenn lowry  uh he is the merton p staltz professor  at brown university in the united states  but he was also the first  african-american tenured professor  of economics in the history of harvard  university back in 1982.  glenn's vehement criticism of  affirmative action  led to him becoming extremely well known  and highly regarded  on the intellectual right from the 1980s  onward  then we have george mapanga who's better  known to many as george the poet  an award-winning podcaster spoken word  artist and cultural critic  george first gained prominence as part  of a new generation of spoken word  artists challenging the traditionalism  of poetry recital  and you can hear his podcast called have  you heard george's podcast  on bbc sounds and dawn butler  is labour mp for brent central and she  served as  shadow women and equality secretary and  shadow minister  for diverse communities now  in the traditional if there is such a  thing traditional how light gets in way  we're going to start by asking each of  our speakers to give us up to three  minutes in which they address a  particular question  and the question to start us off with is  should we be seeking to eradicate  race from social judgments and i wonder  if we could start with you glenn lowry  over to you for three minutes  very well then uh let me be provocative  yes as my answer to that question  uh we should be seeking to eradicate  race from social judgments  now i don't say that this will happen  immediately i don't say that we should  pretend that race doesn't exist  or that we should ignore the history of  racial oppression slavery discrimination  and so on  but what should we be seeking to do as a  society over the long run  we should be seeking to transcend racial  categorization  these are ways of looking at ourselves  which we inherit from the past  we need not be imprisoned by them this  is a conception of the person which at  some level  is superficial it defines us in terms of  the color of our skin the texture of our  hair the shape of bones in our face  that's not who we are  that's our packaging now  i acknowledge that as a practical matter  given the conditions on the ground in  many countries in the world  the legacies of racial oppression  require redress  effective redress cannot be accomplished  without some kind of cognizance of  racial identity  but that's the legacy of the past  we should be attempting to transcend it  if we believe in racial justice if we  genuinely believe in racial equality  the long run solution to this problem is  to subvert  these categories altogether to be  miscegenation uh mongers to marry across  racial lines to integrate our  communities to assimilate our values one  to another across the racial divide  i think history gives us plenty of  reason to expect that the  society built on the foundation of  trans-racial humanism  is a more just and equal society than  the many tribal societies that we see  around the world today where conflict  inequality  and enmity fester so i'm in favor as a  long-run goal  not something achieved tomorrow but  something to keep our eye on  of trying to move to a condition where  we eradicate race from social judgments  glenn larry thank you very much indeed  for that very succinct uh and  provocative opening statement  and we will turn immediately for the  next statement to  george george the purge please go ahead  i don't think we should be seeking to  eradicate race from our  social judgments the difference is  between  us on a physical level are superficial  on a social level they've been exploited  to justify  uh practices and social orders which  are imbalanced and unfortunately that  has shaped our present  race is here and what we do with it is  completely up to us  i am of the belief that what we will not  do  is eliminate it we will not erase it  from the social memory  and we will not get any better at  denying the reality  of racism  we can only look to begin explaining  how racism persists and  we can seek to undermine the mechanisms  of racism  i envision a society in which we are all  so well versed in our history  in the bits of our history that we can  claw back in the  parts of our current existence that we  can verify  person to person i look forward to a  society  in which our young people are versed on  inequality  our young people learn in school the mec  the mechanics of power  and the persistence of social injustice  i anticipate this future and i see it as  essential  in providing an equal footing in which  learners can leave the education process  enter the workforce build healthy happy  functional lives productive lives in  which they feel that the  consequence of their work reflects the  essence of who they  are and the only way that i believe the  society can be reached  is by digging into our inequalities  digging into the archives and  understanding  how the differences between us  geographic  superficial physical  situational and otherwise have been  manipulated  have been exploited to leave us living  in conditions today  in which racial inequality pervades  every element  of our existence we should get better at  talking about this  we should we should have formalized  processes we should have institutions  that reflect this understanding and we  should be brave  in passing the baton onto the generation  following us  giving them the faith to believe that  they can lead us to a better tomorrow  but we will not do that by pretending  race  does not exist thank you very much uh  indeed uh george and again  extremely concise extremely potent and  provocative in the right sort of  way dawn could i turn to you for our  third  opening statement thank you rana and  thank you to both glenn and george  for those opening statements and  um i agree with george  um it would be utopia if  uh race was not a part of  um the remarks the actions  in society but the fact is this race is  a social  uh construct but racism is very much  real and tangible and dangerous  the fact of the matter is that racism  exists  discrimination exists and biases exist  if we do not acknowledge it or measure  it we cannot take  action on it and to ignore race is to  ignore  uh the stories and journeys of  each and every african caribbean asian  minority ethnic people  and communities and we have to accept  and acknowledge  the journeys biases has consequences  and if we do not recognize that we  cannot measure it and we cannot mitigate  against it and that is why we're going  on this journey at the moment it's  very difficult at the moment for some  it's a very uncomfortable conversation  for some  it's a very confronting and challenging  conversation for some  but it's a necessary conversation what  surprises me that people are surprised  they're surprised at the lived  experiences of people of color  and i often wonder why are they  surprised it's because  they live in a different world in a  different bubble where  they are not subjected to everyday  lived racism just because of the color  of one's skin  so i would say for those who try their  best to pretend that they don't see  color they will often then fail to see  the injustices  that comes with being a person of color  and if we're going to move beyond that  we have to just talk frankly about it  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

